        "author": "<LINKEDIN_ORG_URN>",
        "commentary": "The gender pay gap isn't closing. It's widening.\n\nDespite the optimism many HR conversations carry — that pay equity is improving — the data tells a different story. As one expert put it plainly on a recent panel: «unfortunately, the gap is growing.» That's not a policy failure so much as a sign we're intervening in the wrong place.\n\nHere's the trap: when we treat pay inequity as a solved problem and apply surface-level fixes, the underlying issue hardens and resurfaces as something bigger. In the US, for example, once pay equity becomes politicized, it stops being a workplace policy question and turns into a full-blown culture war.\n\nFor payroll and compensation decisions, the starting point can't be good intentions — it has to be measurable data. Otherwise every step we take to 'fix' the gap ends up entrenching it further.\n\nWhat data is driving your organization's pay equity decisions?\n\nhttps://www.datassist.com.tr/?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=social_organic&utm_campaign=datassist_clips&utm_content=c04&utm_term=data_number&yvc_v=A&yvc_lang=en\n\n#ÜcretEşitliği #İK #Bordro #GenderPayGap #VeriYönetimi",
